module test.claim.opaque_host_call_reach_control_witness

import std.types { String, Bool }
import v2.std.live_tree { LiveTreeDisposition, SubstrateInputsOnly }

data live_tree_disposition: LiveTreeDisposition = SubstrateInputsOnly

// THE POSITIVE CONTROL FOR THE PREEMPTION-REACHABILITY COLUMN, AND IT IS THE ONLY REASON THIS
// WITNESS EXISTS.
//
// gunbc#10319 added a `preemption_reachability` column to the floor's per-claim cost receipt,
// recorded at `eval_builtin_inner` and armed from `gunbc.v1_interpreter_opaque_host_call`'s
// grounded surface. On its own first run every one of 3601 routed claims reported
// `cooperatively_pollable` and NOT ONE reported `opaque_host_call_unbounded` -- and a column that
// returns a single value for 100% of its population is indistinguishable from a constant. The
// enrolled test asserting that an UNARMED surface reports UNKNOWN guards a different property
// (that the reader refuses when ungrounded); it does not show the recorder can emit the opaque
// arm on a real claim.
//
// WHY THE UNIFORMITY IS EXPLAINED BUT NOT DISCHARGED BY THAT EXPLANATION. The one claim the §4b
// rung drop measured entering an opaque arm --
// `test.claim.long.root_d_checkpoint_scalar_declared_arity_witness_holds`, recorded at 60317ms
// inside `compile_dag_rust_emit_check` -- is `declined_outside_gate_closure`, `not_executed` in
// the required floor's own disposition artifact. So the opaque population is absent from this
// LANE, which explains the uniform column and demonstrates nothing about the recorder. DESIGN
// §4b: a state unrepresentable in the accepted corpus may still be representable as source handed
// to the compiler by a fixture, and where the RED is authorable, declining the evidence is
// specification-without-execution.
//
// WHAT THIS WITNESS DOES. `compile_dag_rust_emit_check` is a `free_call.*` arm on that grounded
// surface. Calling it puts this claim's cost inside a synchronous native builtin that never
// re-enters `eval_expr`, so no stride point can fall inside it -- which is the definition of the
// `OpaqueHostCallUnbounded` arm. The claim is authored at `test.claim.*` deliberately: that
// prefix is ROUTED by the required floor (796 of 3601 routed claims in the gunbc#10319 run),
// whereas `test.claim.long.*` is declined -- so a control authored there would emit no row at all,
// which is a different failure that looks identical.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT DO, STATED SO NOBODY READS MORE INTO IT. A `test fn` cannot assert its own
// reachability classification: the recorder is read by the floor after the claim returns, not by
// the claim. So the control is TWO PARTS -- this witness establishes that a listed arm is
// REACHED, and the evidence is the row for this identity in `required_floor_claim_cost.tsv`
// reading `opaque_host_call_unbounded`. Reading that row is the executing half; a green here is
// only the reaching half. Automating the join is a separate and larger piece of work.
//
// NEXT TRIGGER, NAMED AS THE CAPABILITY AND WITH WHAT IT MUST BE SUFFICIENT FOR: the floor
// ASSERTS A DECLARED REACHABILITY FOR A NAMED IDENTITY AND REFUSES ON MISMATCH -- sufficient for
// a regression from `opaque_host_call_unbounded` to `cooperatively_pollable` on this identity
// being a RED rather than a silent return to a constant column. "Add an artifact join" is the
// artifact-grained version and is satisfied by a join that reads the wrong column.
//
// Until that exists, the evidence here is a READ AT A MOMENT and is labelled as one. That is
// sufficient to establish that the recorder CAN emit the opaque arm -- the proposition the
// uniform column cannot support -- and it is NOT sufficient to keep a merge-gate relaxation
// honest, because the moment a gate stops blocking a population on this column's word, the
// column's ability to say otherwise becomes load-bearing forever. §4b(4) keeps a positive
// control ENROLLED as executing evidence; a TSV read once is not enrolled. The executed join
// therefore belongs in the PR that changes blocking behaviour, not in front of it.
//
// The assertion below is a real one rather than a pretext: the probe module must emit its own
// function signature and must not emit the algebra head its Nat alias resolves to. A control
// whose body asserted nothing would be a cost with no verdict, and the first cleanup would delete
// it as an artifact with no consumer.
test fn w_opaque_arm_is_reached_and_its_probe_emits() -> Bool {
  compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    "module probe_opaque_reach_control\n\nimport std.nat { Nat }\n\nfn probe_identity(n: Nat) -> Nat {\n  n\n}\n",
    "src/probe_opaque_reach_control.rs",
    ["-> Nat"],
    ["CommutativeSemiring"]
  )
}
